            "Back injury ends more nursing careers than any disease, and most of it comes from lifting patients the way the nurse's mother lifted sacks. <strong>Body mechanics</strong> is the coordinated use of the body to move with the least strain: a <strong>wide base</strong> with the feet apart and one foot forward; the <strong>centre of gravity low</strong> with the hips and knees bent and the back straight; the load held <strong>close to the body</strong>; the <strong>large muscles</strong> of the thighs and buttocks doing the work rather than the small ones of the back; <strong>no twisting</strong> — the feet turn, the trunk does not; the <strong>bed at hip height</strong> and the brakes on; <strong>pushing and pulling</strong> rather than lifting, and sliding rather than either; the move <strong>planned</strong>, explained and counted ('ready, steady, slide') so that two nurses move at once and the patient helps; and the patient's own strength used wherever it exists, which also preserves it. A nurse who is tired, pregnant, small or alone is not an excuse for a bad lift — she is a reason to fetch help and equipment.",
